Reasons to switch to direct rotate at end of rename:
- Pool conservation: rename reuses the SAME key (rotate → rename → rotate). No new key consumed. This is the entire point of the handoff: "kho key dùng lại" — the RENAME flow itself shouldn't burn pool slots... well, actually only when reissuing under a new name. Old flow DELETED then created (net +0... old key deleted from pool, new key took its place — net 0 as well! Hmm interesting: old: delete old + create new = pool count unchanged. New-POST-reissue: old key stays in pool (renamed, unlinked) + new key created = pool count +1 PER RENAME!! That's WORSE than old behavior for pool pressure!).
- D5 also says: key đổi tên → luôn rotate — exactly: rename is always followed by rotate.
- Risk of POST same-name collision on real AQP → possible 409 → reconcile finds renamed key with push null → SVC 'chưa xác nhận kết quả tạo' → RENAME BROKEN. Though... hmm, whether AQP allows duplicate names is unverified (task item 5). If AQP allows duplicates, POST-reissue works but wastes a slot. If not, it breaks. Direct rotate works in BOTH cases (rotate doesn't reference name). 

DECISION CONFIRMED: switch to direct rotate. The old test asserted createStreamKey reissue `{app:'live', stream:'camera_new'}` — new behavior asserts rotateStreamKey called twice (pre + post rename).

